### What makes a JWT self-contained?

Everything needed to understand and trust the token travels inside it, so a service can validate without a central session store. This is what makes JWTs ideal for stateless microservices authentication.

```java
package com.bank.jwt;

import java.util.Base64;

public class JwtStructure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String jwt = "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9" +
                ".eyJzdWIiOiJyYWplc2giLCJyb2xlcyI6WyJDVVNUT01FUiJdLCJpYXQiOjE3MDUzMTIyMDAsImV4cCI6MTcwNTMxMjUwMH0" +
                ".signature_here";

        String[] parts = jwt.split("\\.");
        System.out.println("JWT has 3 parts separated by dots:");
        System.out.println("  Part 1 (Header):    " + decode(parts[0]));
        System.out.println("  Part 2 (Payload):   " + decode(parts[1]));
        System.out.println("  Part 3 (Signature): [cryptographic signature]");
    }

    private static String decode(String base64Url) {
        return new String(Base64.getUrlDecoder().decode(base64Url));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
JWT has 3 parts separated by dots:
  Part 1 (Header):    {"alg":"HS256","typ":"JWT"}
  Part 2 (Payload):   {"sub":"rajesh","roles":["CUSTOMER"],"iat":1705312200,"exp":1705312500}
  Part 3 (Signature): [cryptographic signature]
```

### What does the header contain?

The header is a small JSON object describing the algorithm and token type.

```java
package com.bank.jwt;

public class JwtHeader {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Header fields:");
        System.out.println("  alg: HS256 (HMAC with SHA-256) or RS256 (RSA with SHA-256)");
        System.out.println("  typ: JWT");
        System.out.println("  kid: key-2024-01 (optional, for key rotation)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("HS256: shared secret, both sides can sign and verify");
        System.out.println("RS256: private key signs, public key verifies");
        System.out.println("  -> Better for multi-service: services only need public key");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Header fields:
  alg: HS256 (HMAC with SHA-256) or RS256 (RSA with SHA-256)
  typ: JWT
  kid: key-2024-01 (optional, for key rotation)

HS256: shared secret, both sides can sign and verify
RS256: private key signs, public key verifies
  -> Better for multi-service: services only need public key
```

### What claims does the payload contain?

Claims are statements about the subject and the token. There are three kinds: registered, public, and private.

```java
package com.bank.jwt;

public class JwtPayloadClaims {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Registered claims (standard short names):");
        System.out.println("  iss: issuer (e.g. auth.nbi.com)");
        System.out.println("  sub: subject (e.g. rajesh)");
        System.out.println("  aud: audience (e.g. transfer-service)");
        System.out.println("  exp: expiry time (epoch seconds)");
        System.out.println("  iat: issued at time");
        System.out.println("  nbf: not before time");
        System.out.println("  jti: unique token ID");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Public claims (agreed-upon names):");
        System.out.println("  roles: [CUSTOMER, TELLER]");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Private claims (app-specific):");
        System.out.println("  tenant: NBI-MUMBAI");
        System.out.println("  tier: PREMIUM");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Registered claims (standard short names):
  iss: issuer (e.g. auth.nbi.com)
  sub: subject (e.g. rajesh)
  aud: audience (e.g. transfer-service)
  exp: expiry time (epoch seconds)
  iat: issued at time
  nbf: not before time
  jti: unique token ID

Public claims (agreed-upon names):
  roles: [CUSTOMER, TELLER]

Private claims (app-specific):
  tenant: NBI-MUMBAI
  tier: PREMIUM
```

### How does the signature work?

The signature is computed over the encoded header and payload with a key. A verifier recomputes it and compares; if they differ, the token was altered or forged.

```java
package com.bank.jwt;

public class JwtSignature {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Signature computation:");
        System.out.println("  input = base64Url(header) + '.' + base64Url(payload)");
        System.out.println("  signature = HMACSHA256(input, secretKey)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Verification:");
        System.out.println("  1. Recompute signature from received header.payload");
        System.out.println("  2. Compare with received signature");
        System.out.println("  3. If different -> token was TAMPERED or FORGED -> reject");
        System.out.println("  4. Also check: exp (not expired), iss (correct issuer), aud (correct audience)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Signature computation:
  input = base64Url(header) + '.' + base64Url(payload)
  signature = HMACSHA256(input, secretKey)

Verification:
  1. Recompute signature from received header.payload
  2. Compare with received signature
  3. If different -> token was TAMPERED or FORGED -> reject
  4. Also check: exp (not expired), iss (correct issuer), aud (correct audience)
```

### Can you store sensitive information in the payload?

No. The payload is only Base64Url-encoded, not encrypted. Anyone holding the token can decode and read every claim.

```java
package com.bank.jwt;

import java.util.Base64;

public class PayloadReadable {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // This is a real JWT payload, encoded but NOT encrypted
        String encodedPayload = "eyJzdWIiOiJyYWplc2giLCJiYWxhbmNlIjo5NTAwMDB9";
        String decoded = new String(Base64.getUrlDecoder().decode(encodedPayload));

        System.out.println("Encoded payload: " + encodedPayload);
        System.out.println("Decoded payload: " + decoded);
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("ANYONE can decode this. No secret needed.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("NEVER put in JWT payload:");
        System.out.println("  - Passwords");
        System.out.println("  - Full card numbers");
        System.out.println("  - Government IDs (Aadhaar, PAN)");
        System.out.println("  - Account balances");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("SAFE to put in JWT payload:");
        System.out.println("  - Subject (username)");
        System.out.println("  - Roles");
        System.out.println("  - Issuer, audience, expiry");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Encoded payload: eyJzdWIiOiJyYWplc2giLCJiYWxhbmNlIjo5NTAwMDB9
Decoded payload: {"sub":"rajesh","balance":950000}

ANYONE can decode this. No secret needed.
```

### What happens if someone tries to tamper with the token?

Changing any claim, like altering the role from CUSTOMER to ADMIN, breaks the signature. The verifier rejects the token.

```java
package com.bank.jwt;

public class TamperDetection {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Tamper attempt:");
        System.out.println("  Original payload: {sub: rajesh, roles: [CUSTOMER]}");
        System.out.println("  Attacker changes: {sub: rajesh, roles: [ADMIN]}");
        System.out.println("  Attacker re-encodes payload in Base64Url");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Verifier recomputes signature over new header.payload");
        System.out.println("  Recomputed signature != original signature");
        System.out.println("  -> Token REJECTED: signature mismatch");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  The attacker cannot forge the signature without the secret key");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Tamper attempt:
  Original payload: {sub: rajesh, roles: [CUSTOMER]}
  Attacker changes: {sub: rajesh, roles: [ADMIN]}
  Attacker re-encodes payload in Base64Url

  Verifier recomputes signature over new header.payload
  Recomputed signature != original signature
  -> Token REJECTED: signature mismatch

  The attacker cannot forge the signature without the secret key
```

### How does HS256 compare to RS256?

| Aspect | HS256 | RS256 |
|---|---|---|
| Algorithm | HMAC with SHA-256 | RSA with SHA-256 |
| Key type | Shared secret | Private key signs, public key verifies |
| Who can sign | Anyone with the secret | Only the auth server (private key holder) |
| Who can verify | Anyone with the secret | Any service (public key) |
| Best for | Single service or trusted group | Multi-service distributed systems |
| Risk | If a service leaks the secret, tokens can be forged | Public key can be shared safely |

```java
package com.bank.jwt;

public class Hs256VsRs256 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("HS256: shared secret");
        System.out.println("  Auth server signs with secret");
        System.out.println("  Every service needs the SAME secret to verify");
        System.out.println("  Risk: leaked secret -> anyone can mint tokens");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("RS256: asymmetric");
        System.out.println("  Auth server signs with PRIVATE key");
        System.out.println("  Services verify with PUBLIC key (from JWKS endpoint)");
        System.out.println("  Services CANNOT mint tokens -> safer in multi-service");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
HS256: shared secret
  Auth server signs with secret
  Every service needs the SAME secret to verify
  Risk: leaked secret -> anyone can mint tokens

RS256: asymmetric
  Auth server signs with PRIVATE key
  Services verify with PUBLIC key (from JWKS endpoint)
  Services CANNOT mint tokens -> safer in multi-service
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Tokens cannot be easily revoked once issued. Keep them short-lived with refresh rotation. Validate aud to prevent replay. Always send over TLS.

```text
Senior JWT guidelines:
  [x] Short-lived access tokens (5 minutes)
  [x] Refresh token rotation (detect reuse)
  [x] Validate iss, aud, exp on every request
  [x] RS256 for multi-service systems
  [x] JWKS endpoint for public key distribution
  [x] Always over TLS (tokens are bearer credentials)
  [x] Keep payload lean (bandwidth on every request)
  [x] JWE only if you truly need encrypted claims
```
